\title{Evaluation of energy consumption in metaheuristics}

% \author{\authorname{JJ Merelo\sup{1,2}\orcidAuthor{0000-0002-1385-9741}, Cecilia Merelo Molina\sup{3}\orcidAuthor{0000-0002-5902-0159}, Mario García-Valdez\sup{4} and Carlos Cotta\sup{5}\orcidAuthor{0000-0001-8478-7549}}
% \affiliation{\sup{1}Dept. of Computer Engineering, Automatics and Robotics, University of Granada, Spain}
% \affiliation{\sup{2}CITIC, UGR, Spain}
% \affiliation{\sup{3}Zenzorrito, Granada, Spain}
% \affiliation{\sup{4}Tecnológico Nacional de México, Tijuana, Mexico}
% \affiliation{\sup{5}ITIS Software, Universidad de Málaga, Spain}
% \email{jmerelo@ugr.es}
% }
\author{Hidden authors}

\keywords{Green Computing, Energy Profiling Methodologies, Metaheuristics.}

\abstract{Making an algorithm more effective often involves a series of trade-offs where performance or energy consumption is traded for proximity to the optimum. The question arises of whether it is possible to minimize the cost of achieving new fitness levels by better understanding how and why the algorithm spends energy. In this paper, we are looking for this {\em very cheap lunch} by modelling energy consumption in terms not only of algorithm implementation and its parameters, but also of framework overhead and operating conditions. To this end, we define a measurement protocol that includes all these conditions and perform a statistical analysis addressing the influence and interaction effects of all parameters (both algorithmic and operating) and changes (in parametrization or implementation) under scrutiny. Applying this model to an evolutionary algorithm with a stratified population,
we attain an algorithmic solution that reaches an optimum at a very low cost or
approximates it spending much less energy than others evaluated.}

\onecolumn
\maketitle
\normalsize
\setcounter{footnote}{0}
\vfill
