\section{\uppercase{Introduction and State of the Art}}

An algorithm is an abstract construct: it exists independently of any particular hardware platform or programming language. However, once an algorithm is implemented and executed on a physical machine, energy consumption becomes an unavoidable concern. Cotta et al. \cite{cotta25} demonstrated that the operating context (i.e., the set of environmental and system-level conditions under which a program runs, such as CPU temperature, clock frequency, and competing processes) significantly influences the measured energy consumption of any algorithm's implementation. %% Make this more compact -Carlos
When implementing a metaheuristic, adapting it, or tweaking its parameters, we are usually aiming for efficiency in both performance and energy consumption if possible. However, relying on energy measurements to take decisions requires the latter to be reliable and reproducible.

A common approach in this context has been to try to eliminate the dependence of energy measurements on the operating context \cite{merelo25,bna25-anon}. %% swaps references to use natural order -Carlos
%% Saving orphan lines -Carlos
In this sense, many authors have proposed fixing the temperature, lock clock frequencies and fix the core \cite{pereira2017energy} where the process is going to be running, performing any other kind of operation like a cool-down period after every run (suggested, for instance, by \cite{cotta25}) or simply running exclusively the implementation they are interested in \cite{MANCEBO2021106560}. However, the main issue remains: in a real environment like the one most scientists can afford, i.e., a desktop computer, the operating context will influence results in complex ways. Furthermore, there is a second issue: the way the implementation will influence that same operating environment, creating hysteresis-like effects \cite{cotta25}. %% Add missing backslash -Carlos

We consider a different approach: to include the operating context variables in
performance models, so that we can understand how they influence energy consumption and how they interact with algorithm parameters; in general, we consider operating variables (mainly temperature, but also time needed to run which is impacted by power-saving measures) a {\em covariant} with the energy consumed, and modeled as such. By understanding these interactions, we could pave the way to minimize the effect of these context variables on energy consumption, and make informed decisions about how to design algorithms and implementations that are more energy-efficient, while still achieving good fitness levels. Because we should not forget that the objective of a metaheuristic is to optimize fitness, and we need to understand the tradeoffs involved in saving energy while still reaching a fitness level that is acceptable. By studying  implementation and algorithmic issues together, we can take informed decisions on the kind of direction we want to carry our algorithm implementations.

In this work, we take steps in this direction, %% termino de editar -Carlos
looking at understanding how we can use the models to make decisions about changes, and how such changes, whether at the algorithmic, implementation, or parametric level, can influence energy consumption and the level of fitness achieved. The main research question is the kind of decisions, and at what level, that can help us to get a very cheap lunch, that is, a slightly worse fitness with much less energy consumption, or a much better fitness with slightly more energy consumption. As an auxiliary research question, we would like to know whether there is, in fact, a free lunch: better fitness with less energy, and what contexts would favor such a situation.

The rest of the paper is organized as follows: next we present
background and previous work in Sect. \ref{sec:soa}; then, we proceed to
describe the experimental design (Sect. \ref{sec:bna}), followed by the experimental results
(Sect. \ref{sec:exp});
finally, we discuss these results and draw our conclusions in Sect. \ref{sec:conclusions}.

\section{\uppercase{State of the Art}}\label{sec:soa}

Recent work in green computing has focused on understanding how different factors influence the total energy consumption of computational workloads, particularly in demanding applications such as large-scale deep learning training and inference workloads \cite{fernandez2025energy}. These factors include compilation strategies, hardware platforms, parallelization techniques, and architectural design choices.

In parallel, accurately measuring energy consumption remains a critical challenge. Hardware-based methodologies, such as the SPECpower framework \cite{von2018measuring}, provide reliable measurements but require calibrated external power meters, limiting their accessibility and reproducibility for researchers working on commodity hardware under realistic conditions.
% Saving orphan lines -Carlos
%In contrast to
Unlike controlled laboratory environments, many experiments are conducted on non-dedicated systems (e.g., personal computers and laptops), where background processes and system services are continuously active. As a result, the execution context is inherently dynamic, introducing variability that cannot be fully controlled or isolated. Consequently, energy measurements are influenced not only by the workload under study, but also by system-level factors such as thermal conditions and CPU scheduling behavior.

Prior work has shown that these effects operate at multiple levels. Thermal management mechanisms, such as dynamic voltage and frequency scaling (DVFS) and power gating, continuously adapt processor behavior in response to temperature variations, directly affecting energy consumption \cite{rafsanjani2025thermal}. Many of these mechanisms are implemented at the hardware level and operate independently of the operating system (OS),
%% acronym to be used later -Carlos
making their effects difficult to observe or control explicitly. At the same time, operating system scheduling decisions influence energy consumption, as the power profile of a workload depends on the type of processor cycles executed and their distribution across CPU cores \cite{jaiantilal2010modeling}.

Taken together, these observations indicate that energy consumption is governed by a combination of hardware-driven adaptations and software-level decisions that evolve dynamically during execution. While prior work has extensively explored these factors from a modeling and optimization perspective, their methodological implications for energy measurement remain underexplored.
% Saving orphan lines -Carlos
%From a methodological
From this latter standpoint, this dynamic behavior introduces significant challenges for achieving reliable and reproducible measurements, as system state and environmental conditions can bias experimental results. In particular, recent studies have identified a hysteretic effect in system behavior, whereby the system remains in a high-energy-consumption state even after a workload has completed \cite{cotta25}. This effect is strongly influenced by thermal dynamics, as the temperature reached during one experimental run can significantly affect subsequent measurements \cite{cotta24}. Furthermore, the relationship between temperature and power consumption is non-linear, ranging from quadratic behavior in moderate ranges \cite{devogeleer2014modelingtemperaturebiaspower} to exponential growth at higher temperatures \cite{PENG2025104837}.

Despite these advances, existing approaches often overlook the dynamic and non-deterministic nature of real-world execution environments. Energy measurements are affected by temporal dependencies, thermal effects, and variability
% Saving orphan lines -Carlos
%introduced by both hardware-level mechanisms and operating system
resulting from hardware mechanisms and OS
behavior, including process scheduling and task migration across CPU cores. These factors can lead to inconsistent and non-reproducible results, particularly on non-dedicated systems.

In this work, we address this challenge by proposing a measurement methodology that incorporates controlled waiting intervals to manage these dynamic effects. By stabilizing thermal and system conditions prior to measurement, our approach reduces variability and enables more consistent energy profiling on commodity hardware platforms.

\section{\uppercase{A Brave New Algorithm}} \label{sec:bna}

Far from trying to obscure the meaning of a metaheuristic by using a metaphor \cite{sorensen}, our description of a Brave New Algorithm (BNA), based in the novel Brave New World, by Aldous Huxley \cite{huxley2022brave}, searches to simplify the explanation of a stratified evolutionary algorithm (EA), by giving every {\em stratum} a name that is relatively easy to understand for those familiar with this book.  %% comienzo a editar -Carlos
Thus, we are not proposing a new metaheuristic, but rather a new way of structuring the population and the reproduction process, which can be applied to any metaheuristic that uses a population of individuals, aiming to introduce a new way of balancing exploration and exploitation, as well as a new way of keeping diversity in the population. The algorithm is described in more detail in \cite{merelo2022brave-anon,bna25-anon}, but we will give a brief description here for the sake of completeness.%% termino de editar -Carlos
%% Sorting references by number -Carlos

What BNA introduces with respect to other EAs is the division of the population into {\em castes} (i.e., strata);
each receiving a differentiated treatment for reproduction.
%% tuning down the metaphor -Carlos
%% :-) - JJ
More precisely, reproduction goes as follows by caste:
\begin{itemize}
\item The $\alpha$ caste will mate only with other individuals of the same caste, undergoing mutation and then crossover.
\item The $\beta$ caste will reproduce only with members of the $\alpha$ caste, again with mutation and crossover.
\item The $\delta$ and $\epsilon$ castes will only reproduce via cloning; this clones will then be mutated.
\item The $\gamma$ caste will use hill-climbing for a number of steps to improve its fitness.
\end{itemize}

All individuals will be ranked according to their fitness and then assigned to the castes;
in particular, the $\alpha$ caste will receive the best while the $\gamma$ caste will get only the last ones; percentages assigned to every caste will be an algorithm parameter, although in order to simplify we will only change the $\alpha$ percentage, with $\delta$ and $\epsilon$ getting always 5\%; $\beta$ is always double that of $\alpha$ and finally $\gamma$ the rest; this gives us a single parameter to regulate this aspect of the algorithm, which is strongly related to the exploration/exploitation balance.

The algorithm uses elitism to keep the best member of the previous population
(replacing the worst one) but other than that, all individuals reproduce one way or the other. This is the main difference with respect to other EAs: the worst elements are not dropped from the reproduction pool but mutated in a random or directed way (in the case of $\gamma$). This is intended to keep a diverse population, striking a good exploration/exploitation balance again. This diversity can be enhanced by using differential mutation rates for the different castes;
while
this adds another parameter for experimental design,
%but still
it allows customization for specific problems or problem sizes.
%
%The parameters that can be varied in the algorithm are then
The free parameters are thus:
\begin{itemize}
\item Population size:
%Due to the population structure there are restrictions on the number of individuals; essentially, all castes need to have an integer as size.
Note that all castes need to have a non-zero integer size, which limits the possible values for this parameter.
\item $\alpha$ percentage:
%which is
the essential parameter for controlling the balance between exploitation and exploration.
\item Mutation rates
%for the different castes except $\gamma$, which
(for all castes except $\gamma$): these can be set to the same value or customized for every caste.
\item Local-search depth: maximum number of hill-climbing steps while there is still improvement in the fitness.
\item Stopping criterion: the maximum number of generations without improvement.
\end{itemize}

Note that the orchestrated use of local improvement within this general evolutionary engine makes BNA fall within the general scope of memetic algorithms \cite{cotta2025harnessing}. %
The algorithm is written in Julia \cite{perkel2019julia} and released as open source \cite{Ceci2021bnea-anon}. Julia is a language designed with a JIT compiler to be fast and introduces native concurrent execution. It has been already used in a number of evolutionary algorithm implementations such as \cite{sanchez2023evolp}. In our case, this parallel execution feature matches well the multi-caste evolution of
BNA, opening new venues of research as well as energy-saving. In this paper, however, we are still profiling and optimizing at the single-processor level.

\section{\uppercase{Experiments and Results}}\label{sec:exp}

We have used the following hardware-software stack for the experiments\begin{itemize}
\item We will be running them in an AMD Ryzen 9 9950X 16-Core Processor. This is a two-chiplet CPU with 16 cores and 32 threads in a functionally heterogeneous configuration, with cores power-capped depending on their certified performance. Although architecturally both dies are identical, cores are grouped in descending order of frequency rating, making it in practice a heterogeneous configurarion.
\item We are running Ubuntu 25.10 with kernel {\tt 6.17.0-19-generic}.
\item The frequency scaling driver is set to {\tt amd-pstate-epp} and the scaling governor to {\tt powersave}. The energy performance preference is set to {\tt balance\_performance}, which is the default behavior. This again seeks to reproduce actual researcher configuration.
\item The version of Julia used is 1.11.9. This is not the latest stable release (that would be 1.13), but initial performance tests discarded latest versions.
\item We will be using RAPL counters to measure energy via the {\sf pinpoint} command-line tool \cite{pinpoint} with a sampling period of 0.1 s. For system temperature we will be using {\em sensors} that tap an AMD driver.
\item The experiments will be run from a Perl script, capturing output from {\sf pinpoint} and saving it to a CSV file.
\end{itemize}

For every experiment, we save the following data\begin{itemize}
\item Initial and final temperature for every die.
\item Die where the process is running.
\item PKG (CPU) energy spent, taken from the RAPL counters.
\item Time used.
\end{itemize}

Since every run is affected by the overhead, we will try to isolate the workload by having two types of runs (changed by a command line switch): \begin{itemize}
\item {\em Baseline} runs, which just generate the initial population,
\item and {\em workload} runs which run the algorithm until the end conditions are met.
\end{itemize}

Baseline runs are essentially (implementation-dependent) overhead, so we use a {\em sandwich} strategy where every workload run is {\em sandwiched} between two baseline runs. We will repeat workload run 30 times sandwiched between 31 baseline ($B$) runs, with every $B$ (except first and last) affecting 2 $W$ runs.
For workload runs we capture the fitness of the best individual at the end of the run and the total number of evaluations and generations, additionally to the operating data mentioned above.

We have established another experimental condition, specific for multi-die CPUs, which tries to minimize the effect of temperature on energy consumption: at the beginning of every run, die temperatures are checked and (using {\tt taskset}) the process is always sent to the {\em hottest} die \cite{hot-first-anon}. This process has proved to decrease the influence of the runs on the operating conditions, thus increasing the variance that can be attributed exclusively to the process itself.

For this paper, we have focused on a single problem and a single dimension: The Sphere floating-point fitness function included in the BBOB benchmark \cite{hansen2010comparing} for 10 dimensions. The reason we have not introduced more functions and dimensions is that we wanted to focus on the effect of the operating conditions (measured through running time and temperatures) and the algorithm parameters as well as results (fitness, evaluations). Introducing more degrees of freedom would not give us more insights into this model, and it might confuse the interpretation of results\footnote{In the discussion section we will include how these results can be generalized to other problems and dimensions.}. % We need to check we do this
Statistical analysis, however, provides the certainty we need to draw sound conclusions from this set of experiments.

Our intention is to try, with experiments, to see the dependence of energy consumption on algorithm parameters as well as operating conditions, and the dependence of fitness reached on the latter, in order to check if it is possible at all to reach lower levels of fitness spending less or the same energy. The four parameters that we will be changing are:\begin{itemize}
\item $\alpha$ percentage with two values: 10\% and 25\%. Since it is the main lever for the exploitation/exploration balance, we are looking for better fitness values with less space exploration (less evaluations).
\item The number or {\sf steps} for hill-climbing in the $\gamma$ caste is related to
exploitation. Local search is an intensifying operator, so more steps means more fitness-oriented exploitation.
We will test 16 and 64 steps.
\item The number of generations without improvement to stop or {\sf max\_gens} will be set to 10 and 25; more generations allow more exploration and the possibility to go up a notch in fitness.
\item Population size is going to be set to 400 and 800; larger population means more diversity giving it a better chance to exploit existing values, but also more exploration.
\end{itemize}

This gives us 16 different parameter settings times 30 $W$ and 31 $B$ runs. To ensure we cover different operating conditions, we repeat every set 4 times after at least one reboot; the distance between one set of experiments and the next is random.
We have an open science policy and therefore, all code, experimental results and analyses are available under a free license at our repository \cite{Ceci2021bnea-anon}.

The first batch of experiments will be used as experimental baseline; the mutation rate for every caste will be 40\%, meaning that, on average, 4 components of every individual mutated (whose dimension is 10) will change randomly within a certain range; using this baseline, we will perform different experiments changing algorithm implementation or design.

<<ppsn.baseline, echo=FALSE, message=FALSE, fig.cap="Testing the gamma upgrade against the baseline.", fig.height=3, fig.pos="h!tbp", fig.env="figure*">>=
source("R/process_deltas.R")
library(dplyr)
library(ggplot2)
library(knitr)
library(kableExtra)
library(ggExtra) # for ggMarginal

load("data/ppsn_speedup_fixed_gradient_descent.rds")
load("data/gamma_upgrade.rds")

gamma_upgrade$die <- NULL # eliminate this extra column, we're not using it
ppsn_speedup_fixed_gradient_descent$group <- "baseline"
gamma_upgrade$group <- "gamma_upgrade"
comparison_gamma <- rbind(ppsn_speedup_fixed_gradient_descent, gamma_upgrade)

comparison_gamma_w <- process_deltas(comparison_gamma)

comparison_gamma_w$initial_temp <- (comparison_gamma_w$initial_temp_1 + comparison_gamma_w$initial_temp_2)/2
comparison_gamma_w$final_temp <- (comparison_gamma_w$final_temp_1 + comparison_gamma_w$final_temp_2)/2

comparison_gg <- ggplot(comparison_gamma_w, aes(x = diff_fitness, y = delta_PKG, fill=group,color=group)) +
  geom_point(alpha=0.5) +
  labs(x = "Diff Fitness",
    y = "Delta PKG"
  ) + theme(legend.position = "bottom") + theme_minimal()

ggMarginal(comparison_gg, type="density", groupFill=T, alpha=0.5)

# Raw (unadjusted) differences between the gamma upgrade and the baseline
gamma_group_means <- comparison_gamma_w %>%
  group_by( group ) %>%
  summarise( mean_delta_PKG = mean(delta_PKG),
             mean_delta_seconds = mean(delta_seconds),
             mean_evaluations = mean(evaluations),
             mean_initial_temp = mean(initial_temp),
             mean_final_temp = mean(final_temp),
             mean_diff_fitness = mean(diff_fitness),
             .groups = "drop" )
gamma_raw_difference <- function(column) {
  gamma_group_means[[column]][gamma_group_means$group == "gamma_upgrade"] -
    gamma_group_means[[column]][gamma_group_means$group == "baseline"]
}

# Does the upgrade change the fitness reached, all parameters being equal?
gamma_fitness_model <- glm( diff_fitness ~ group + steps*max_gens*population_size*alpha, data = comparison_gamma_w )
@

In order to test the influence of configuration changes in fitness and energy, we will perform one single change: we will change the rank of the $\gamma$ caste from the last slot to the third one; that is, instead of applying hill-climbing to the chromosomes with the lowest fitness, it will be applied to those with a fitness in the middle. A priori, that would seem like a way of speeding up exploitation by carrying a population with a decent fitness up the ladder, thus reducing the number of evaluations. The results can be observed in Figure \ref{fig:ppsn.baseline}, where we plot the two measures we are interested in, the workload PKG energy, measured as a difference to the $B$ runs
and the difference to an optimal fitness; in the margins the distribution for the two measures is also plotted. There seems to be no change in the distribution of fitness; however, energy spent is distributed along a single peak for the baseline, while there are other small peaks for higher energy consumption for the
alleged $\gamma$ upgrade.

<<ppsn.gamma.model, message=FALSE, echo=FALSE, fig.cap="Model for the gamma upgrade.", fig.height=4, fig.pos="h!tbp">>=

# Why does the gamma upgrade spend more energy? We follow the causal chain
#   group -> evaluations -> running time -> final temperature -> delta_PKG
# and add the mediators one at a time to the energy model, watching how much of
# the gamma effect each one absorbs. initial_temp is measured before the run
# starts, so it is a covariate (operating context), not a mediator, and it is in
# every model. The group enters additively (no group:evaluations interaction):
# with an interaction, the group coefficient is the effect at zero evaluations,
# which is what the previous version of this model was reporting.
gamma_effect <- function(model, term = "groupgamma_upgrade") {
  coefficients_table <- summary(model)$coefficients
  estimate <- coefficients_table[term, "Estimate"]
  standard_error <- coefficients_table[term, "Std. Error"]
  c( estimate = estimate,
     lower = estimate - qnorm(0.975) * standard_error,
     upper = estimate + qnorm(0.975) * standard_error,
     p_value = coefficients_table[term, "Pr(>|t|)"] )
}

gamma_energy_total_model <- glm( delta_PKG ~ group + initial_temp + steps*max_gens*population_size*alpha, data = comparison_gamma_w )
gamma_energy_evaluations_model <- update( gamma_energy_total_model, . ~ . + evaluations )
gamma_energy_time_model <- update( gamma_energy_evaluations_model, . ~ . + delta_seconds )
gamma_energy_temperature_model <- update( gamma_energy_time_model, . ~ . + final_temp )

gamma_energy_models <- list( gamma_energy_total_model,
                             gamma_energy_evaluations_model,
                             gamma_energy_time_model,
                             gamma_energy_temperature_model )
gamma_energy_effects <- as.data.frame( do.call( rbind, lapply( gamma_energy_models, gamma_effect ) ) )
gamma_total_effect <- gamma_energy_effects$estimate[1]
gamma_mediation_table <- data.frame(
  controlling_for = c( "Parameters and initial temperature only",
                       "+ evaluations",
                       "+ running time",
                       "+ final temperature" ),
  estimate = gamma_energy_effects$estimate,
  interval = sprintf( "[%.1f, %.1f]", gamma_energy_effects$lower, gamma_energy_effects$upper ),
  p_value = format.pval( gamma_energy_effects$p_value, digits = 2, eps = 1e-4 ),
  percentage_of_total = 100 * (1 - gamma_energy_effects$estimate / gamma_total_effect)
)

# Each link of the chain, all parameters being equal
gamma_evaluations_model <- glm( evaluations ~ group + steps*max_gens*population_size*alpha, data = comparison_gamma_w )
gamma_seconds_model <- glm( delta_seconds ~ group + evaluations + initial_temp + steps*max_gens*population_size*alpha, data = comparison_gamma_w )
gamma_final_temp_model <- glm( final_temp ~ group + delta_seconds + evaluations + initial_temp + steps*max_gens*population_size*alpha, data = comparison_gamma_w )

# What the upgrade buys, per configuration: difference (gamma - baseline) in
# median energy and median fitness, with Holm-corrected Wilcoxon p-values.
# Fitness does not change anywhere; energy goes up in most configurations, and
# gamma performs fewer evaluations (see gamma_raw_difference("evaluations")).
gamma_per_configuration <- comparison_gamma_w %>%
  group_by( population_size, alpha, steps, max_gens ) %>%
  summarise( energy_difference = median(delta_PKG[group == "gamma_upgrade"]) - median(delta_PKG[group == "baseline"]),
             fitness_difference = median(diff_fitness[group == "gamma_upgrade"]) - median(diff_fitness[group == "baseline"]),
             evaluations_difference = median(evaluations[group == "gamma_upgrade"]) - median(evaluations[group == "baseline"]),
             energy_p_value = wilcox.test( delta_PKG ~ group, exact = FALSE )$p.value,
             fitness_p_value = wilcox.test( diff_fitness ~ group, exact = FALSE )$p.value,
             .groups = "drop" ) %>%
  mutate( energy_p_holm = p.adjust( energy_p_value, "holm" ),
          fitness_p_holm = p.adjust( fitness_p_value, "holm" ) )

# Is the extra energy spent through evaluations? It is if the upgrade performs
# significantly more evaluations AND adding evaluations to the energy model
# accounts for most (> 50%) of the gamma effect. Wording for the text is built
# here so the sentence stays correct whatever the numbers are.
gamma_evaluations_effect <- gamma_effect( gamma_evaluations_model )
gamma_evaluations_share <- gamma_mediation_table$percentage_of_total[2]
gamma_energy_through_evaluations <- gamma_evaluations_effect["estimate"] > 0 &&
  gamma_evaluations_effect["p_value"] < 0.05 &&
  gamma_evaluations_share > 50
gamma_evaluations_direction <- ifelse( gamma_evaluations_effect["estimate"] > 0, "more", "fewer" )
gamma_evaluations_share_text <- if ( gamma_evaluations_share >= 0 ) {
  paste0( "accounts for ", round(gamma_evaluations_share), "\\% of the extra energy" )
} else {
  paste0( "makes the extra energy ", round(-gamma_evaluations_share), "\\% larger instead of smaller" )
}

# How large is the extra energy in practical terms? Compare the gamma effect
# (first row of the mediation table) with the total energy of a baseline
# workload run (PKG, not delta) and with the run-to-run spread left over once
# group, parameters and initial temperature are accounted for (mostly
# operating conditions).
# "p = 0.18" or "p < 0.0001", for inline use in the text
format_p_value <- function(p_value) {
  if ( p_value < 1e-4 ) "$p < 0.0001$" else paste0( "$p = ", signif(p_value, 2), "$" )
}
gamma_evaluations_difference_text <- if ( gamma_evaluations_effect["p_value"] < 0.05 ) {
  paste0( "performs ", format(abs(round(gamma_evaluations_effect["estimate"])), big.mark = ","), " ",
          gamma_evaluations_direction, " evaluations per run than the baseline" )
} else {
  "does not perform a significantly different number of evaluations than the baseline"
}
gamma_total_effect_p_value <- gamma_energy_effects$p_value[1]
gamma_total_energy_per_run <- mean( comparison_gamma_w$PKG[comparison_gamma_w$group == "baseline"] )
gamma_effect_percentage_of_run <- 100 * gamma_total_effect / gamma_total_energy_per_run
gamma_run_to_run_spread <- sd( residuals( gamma_energy_total_model ) )
gamma_significance_text <- if ( gamma_total_effect_p_value < 0.05 ) "statistically significant but small" else "not statistically significant, and small"

# kable( gamma_mediation_table, digits = c(0, 2, 0, 0, 1), row.names = FALSE,
#        caption = "Extra energy (in J) spent by the gamma upgrade with respect to the baseline, all algorithm parameters being equal, as variables along the evaluations, running time, final temperature chain are added to the model. The last column is the part of the first-row effect that is accounted for by the variables added so far.",
#        col.names = c( "Model controlling for", "Gamma effect (J)", "95\\% CI", "p-value", "\\% of effect accounted for" ),
#        format = "latex", escape = FALSE, table.envir = "table*" ) %>%
#   kable_styling( full_width = F, position = "center" )
@

%% PROPOSAL: On average, a $W$ run of the $\gamma$ upgrade spends \Sexpr{round(gamma_raw_difference("delta_PKG"), 1)} J more than the baseline, and we need to find out why. Energy is spent while the algorithm runs, so the most likely explanation is a chain of effects: the upgrade performs more evaluations; more evaluations take more time ($\Delta R$, where $R$ is the running time); a longer run heats up the CPU, and a hotter CPU draws more power. To check this chain, we start from a model of $\Delta$PKG that includes the group (baseline or $\gamma$ upgrade), the four algorithm parameters (interacting) and the initial temperature (the average of the two dies); the latter is measured before the run starts, so it reflects the operating context, not the run itself. Then we add, one at a time, the variables along the chain: the number of evaluations, $\Delta R$ and the final temperature. If the chain explains the extra energy, the coefficient of the group will shrink as these variables are added; whatever is left at the end is energy the upgrade spends with the same evaluations, running time and temperature, that is, a higher power draw.

The extra energy spent by the $\gamma$ upgrade is \Sexpr{if (gamma_energy_through_evaluations) "" else "not "}due to a larger number of evaluations: all parameters being equal, it \Sexpr{gamma_evaluations_difference_text} (\Sexpr{format_p_value(gamma_evaluations_effect["p_value"])}), and adding the number of evaluations to the energy model
% \Sexpr{gamma_evaluations_share_text} (Table \ref{tab:ppsn.gamma.model}).
does not change the extra energy.
This extra energy, \Sexpr{round(gamma_total_effect, 1)} J (\Sexpr{format_p_value(gamma_total_effect_p_value)}), is \Sexpr{gamma_significance_text}: it is \Sexpr{round(gamma_effect_percentage_of_run, 1)}\% of the \Sexpr{round(gamma_total_energy_per_run)} J that a workload run spends in total, and well below the \Sexpr{round(gamma_run_to_run_spread)} J standard deviation of run-to-run energy left once the algorithm parameters and initial temperature are accounted for, which reflects mostly the operating conditions. This means that we are not buying anything with this change, and losing something: a small but significant amount of energy. We need then to reject this change, even if {\em a priori} it seemed a good idea. So let us try and perform additional changes to the algorithm or parameter values, always looking for better fitness/energy ratios.

A possible fitness-neutral path to optimization is to work on the platform, making it faster, which in principle will it make spend less energy. In the case of Julia, one straightforward way is to look for unused libraries in the dependencies list and eliminate it. That, however, will work on the $B$ runs. In order to also optimize the
$W$ runs we will perform two additional optimizations: reduce memory allocations during caste distribution, and eliminate computing population entropy, a feature that we are not actually using in this case and that involved $p^2$ operations where $p$ is the population size.

<<ppsn.microopt, message=FALSE, echo=FALSE, fig.cap="Comparing baseline with the micro-optimized version of the algorithm.", fig.height=3, fig.pos="!tbp", fig.env="figure*">>=
load("data/ppsn_microopt.rds")

ppsn_speedup_fixed_gradient_descent$group <- "baseline"
ppsn_microopt$group <- "microopt"
ppsn_microopt$die <- NULL
comparison_microopt <- rbind(ppsn_speedup_fixed_gradient_descent, ppsn_microopt)

comparison_microopt_w <- process_deltas(comparison_microopt)

comparison_microopt_w$initial_temp <- (comparison_microopt_w$initial_temp_1 + comparison_microopt_w$initial_temp_2)/2
comparison_microopt_w$final_temp <- (comparison_microopt_w$final_temp_1 + comparison_microopt_w$final_temp_2)/2

comparison_bm <- ggplot(comparison_microopt_w, aes(x = diff_fitness, y = delta_PKG, fill=group,color=group)) +
  geom_point(alpha=0.5) +
  labs(x = "Diff Fitness",
    y = "Delta PKG"
  ) + theme(legend.position = "bottom") + theme_minimal()

ggMarginal(comparison_bm, type="density", groupFill=T, alpha=0.5)

# ggplot(comparison_microopt_w, aes(x = delta_seconds, y = delta_PKG, color=factor(alpha), shape=factor(group))) +
#   geom_point(alpha=0.5) +
#   labs(x = "Delta Seconds",
#     y = "Delta PKG"
#   ) + theme(legend.position = "bottom") + theme_minimal()
@

In Figure \ref{fig:ppsn.microopt} we plot again $\Delta$PKG against the difference in fitness, same as we have done in Figure \ref{fig:ppsn.baseline}. Taking into account that it is a programming-only change, we find that the distribution of fitness as well as the distribution of energy tends to have peaks in an area of lower consumption/better fitness. On one side, this means that we have effectively optimized energy consumption through this optimization; on the other side, programming changes might have fixed some bug that has made fitness better.

<<smartgreens.microopt.energy.fitness, message=FALSE, echo=FALSE>>=
# Energy effort per fitness level: energy-fitness product (median delta_PKG x
# median diff_fitness) and its log10, the energy-fitness score; see
# R/energy_fitness_product.R. Lower is better; one fitness level (10x closer
# to the optimum) at the same energy = 1 less in the score.
# All vectors are named by version: e.g. microopt_energy["microopt"].
source("R/energy_fitness_product.R")
microopt_energy_fitness <- energy_fitness_product( comparison_microopt_w, energy_column = "delta_PKG",
                                                   versions = c("baseline", "microopt") )

microopt_energy               <- setNames( microopt_energy_fitness$energy, microopt_energy_fitness$version )               # median delta_PKG, J
microopt_fitness              <- setNames( microopt_energy_fitness$fitness, microopt_energy_fitness$version )              # median diff_fitness
microopt_fitness_level        <- setNames( microopt_energy_fitness$fitness_level, microopt_energy_fitness$version )        # log10(median diff_fitness)
microopt_energy_fitness_product <- setNames( microopt_energy_fitness$energy_fitness_product, microopt_energy_fitness$version ) # J x diff_fitness
microopt_energy_fitness_score <- setNames( microopt_energy_fitness$energy_fitness_score, microopt_energy_fitness$version )  # log10 of the product

# microopt vs baseline: ratio of the products (0.5 = half the energy for the
# same fitness, or the same energy for half the distance to the optimum) and
# difference of the scores (= log10 of that ratio).
microopt_energy_fitness_change       <- energy_fitness_change( microopt_energy_fitness, "baseline", "microopt" )
microopt_energy_fitness_ratio        <- microopt_energy_fitness_change[["ratio"]]
microopt_energy_fitness_score_change <- microopt_energy_fitness_change[["score_change"]]

# Referenced as Table \ref{tab:smartgreens.microopt.energy.fitness} (label taken from the chunk name)
energy_fitness_kable( microopt_energy_fitness,
  caption = "Median workload energy ($\\Delta$PKG), median distance to the optimum ({\\sf diff\\_fitness}) and energy-fitness score, $\\log_{10}$(energy $\\times$ {\\sf diff\\_fitness}), for the baseline and the micro-optimized version. Lower is better in all columns." )
@

Table \ref{tab:smartgreens.microopt.energy.fitness} shows the median $\Delta$PKG, fitness difference to optimum, as well as a composite {\em fitness-energy score} which is the sum of the 10-base logarithm of both quantities, lower is better; a difference in 1 in the energy or fitness part will mean it is 10x better. In general, this term will be dominated by fitness. In this case the difference is \Sexpr{microopt_energy_fitness_score_change} but it shows anyway an improvement over the old value.

We need to understand better, however, how this new value of energy is achieved; when an experiment runs it has an influence on the operating conditions, especially the temperature.
%
<<smartgreens.microopt.operating.conditions, message=FALSE, echo=FALSE>>=
# How is the lower energy of microopt reached, once we know it is not through
# the number of evaluations (~0 J per evaluation)? Through the operating
# conditions of the run: its running time (delta_seconds, workload minus the
# two sandwiching B runs) and the initial temperature it finds, which the
# previous B run leaves behind. Models and table: R/operating_conditions.R.
source("R/operating_conditions.R")
microopt_operating <- operating_conditions( comparison_microopt_w, "baseline", "microopt" )

# Savings (positive = microopt spends less), same configuration, evaluations
# and initial temperature
microopt_seconds_saved                 <- microopt_operating$seconds_saved                 # s per run
microopt_energy_saved                  <- microopt_operating$energy_saved                  # J per run
microopt_energy_saved_same_time        <- microopt_operating$energy_saved_same_time        # J, lower power
microopt_energy_saved_through_time     <- microopt_operating$energy_saved_through_time     # J, shorter runs
microopt_percentage_saved_through_time <- microopt_operating$percentage_saved_through_time

# Named baseline, microopt, difference_p_value
microopt_power                       <- microopt_operating$power                       # J per s of running time (W)
microopt_seconds_per_degree          <- microopt_operating$seconds_per_degree          # s per degree C
microopt_energy_per_degree           <- microopt_operating$energy_per_degree           # J per degree C, total
microopt_energy_per_degree_same_time <- microopt_operating$energy_per_degree_same_time # J per degree C, same running time
microopt_seconds                     <- microopt_operating$seconds                     # median s
microopt_initial_temp                <- microopt_operating$initial_temp                # median degrees C

# Referenced as Table \ref{tab:smartgreens.microopt.operating.conditions}
operating_conditions_kable( microopt_operating, "baseline", "microopt",
  caption = "Influence of the operating conditions on the energy ($\\Delta$PKG) of a workload run, for each version, with the same algorithm configuration and number of evaluations. Only the values that differ significantly between the two versions ($p < 0.05$) are shown." )
@
%
What we show in Table \ref{tab:smartgreens.microopt.operating.conditions} is how these energy savings are achieved; the main way of changing total energy spent is through running time, but also through the indirect influence in the operating conditions, mainly temperature: higher temperature would mean more energy is spent. The initial temperature for the $W$ (Workload) runs is influenced by all runs in the batch so far, but more directly by the $B$ run that precedes it; in that sense, the {\sf microopt} experiment $W$ runs start at a slightly higher temperature. However, it incurs in less energy expenses by every degree of initial temperature, so the total balance tips towards this new experimental level. The same occurs on the running time side: it runs faster, and it spends less energy for each second. This change, effectively, is the closest we will get to a {\em free ride}: spend less energy for a better fitness. So we will keep it as the new baseline.

<<smartgreens.low.mutation, message=FALSE, echo=FALSE, fig.cap="Comparison of energy consumed vs. final temperature for the microopt and low mutation set of experiments.", fig.height=3, fig.pos="!tbp", fig.env="figure*">>=
source("R/type_two_variance.R")
load("data/ppsn_low_mutation.rds")
load("data/ppsn_microopt.rds") # redo this, we can use the die now

ppsn_low_mutation$group <- "low_mutation"
ppsn_microopt$group <- "microopt"

comparison_microopt_low_mutation <- rbind(ppsn_low_mutation, ppsn_microopt)
comparison_microopt_low_mutation[ startsWith( comparison_microopt_low_mutation$work, "base-"),]$work <- "Base"
comparison_microopt_low_mutation[ !startsWith( comparison_microopt_low_mutation$work, "Base"),]$work <- "Workload"

comparison_microopt_low_mutation %>% group_by( work, group, die ) %>% summarise( count = n(), .groups="drop" ) -> comparison_microopt_low_mutation_summary

comparison_microopt_low_mutation$initial_temp <- (comparison_microopt_low_mutation$initial_temp_1 + comparison_microopt_low_mutation$initial_temp_2 )/ 2
comparison_microopt_low_mutation$final_temp <- (comparison_microopt_low_mutation$final_temp_1 + comparison_microopt_low_mutation$final_temp_2 )/ 2

comparison_microopt_low_mutation %>% group_by( work, group, die ) %>%
  summarise( mean_initial_temp = mean(initial_temp),
             mean_final_temp = mean(final_temp),
             mean_PKG = mean(PKG),
             mean_seconds = mean(seconds),
             .groups="drop" ) -> comparison_microopt_low_mutation_operating_conditions_summary

comparison_microopt_low_mutation$delta_temp <- comparison_microopt_low_mutation$final_temp - comparison_microopt_low_mutation$initial_temp

comparison_microopt_low_mutation_w <- process_deltas(comparison_microopt_low_mutation)

# ggplot(comparison_microopt_low_mutation, aes(x = final_temp, y = PKG, color=group )) +
#   geom_point(alpha=0.5) +
#   facet_wrap(~work) +
#   labs(x = "Final Temperature",
#     y = "PKG Energy"
#   ) + theme(legend.position = "bottom") + theme_minimal()

comparison_micro_low <- ggplot(comparison_microopt_low_mutation_w, aes(x = diff_fitness, y = delta_PKG, fill=group,color=group)) +
  geom_point(alpha=0.5) +
  labs(x = "Diff Fitness",
    y = "Delta PKG"
  ) + theme(legend.position = "bottom") + theme_minimal()

ggMarginal(comparison_micro_low, type="density", groupFill=T, alpha=0.5)
@

After this first optimization at the framework layer, we will make an additional change that concerns the algorithm itself. While the mutation rate was set at 40\%, in this case, we will lower it to 10\%, meaning that when changing a chromosome, only one element of the chromosome will be changed. While this might seem an obvious way of reducing random search, (i.e., exploration),
what we are interested in is the way it will affect the operating conditions first, and then obviously the energy consumed as well as the fitness reached. This is why we compare in Figure \ref{fig:smartgreens.low.mutation} what is the distribution of energy consumption and fitness per run. The initial impression is that fitness reaches a totally different level, although energy spent is distributed in a different way, with two peaks at higher energy levels than {\sf microopt}.
%
<<smartgreens.low.mutation.energy.fitness, message=FALSE, echo=FALSE>>=
# Same as smartgreens.microopt.energy.fitness, microopt as the reference.
# All vectors are named by version: e.g. low_mutation_energy["low_mutation"].
low_mutation_energy_fitness <- energy_fitness_product( comparison_microopt_low_mutation_w, energy_column = "delta_PKG",
                                                       versions = c("microopt", "low_mutation") )

low_mutation_energy                 <- setNames( low_mutation_energy_fitness$energy, low_mutation_energy_fitness$version )                 # median delta_PKG, J
low_mutation_fitness                <- setNames( low_mutation_energy_fitness$fitness, low_mutation_energy_fitness$version )                # median diff_fitness
low_mutation_fitness_level          <- setNames( low_mutation_energy_fitness$fitness_level, low_mutation_energy_fitness$version )          # log10(median diff_fitness)
low_mutation_energy_fitness_product <- setNames( low_mutation_energy_fitness$energy_fitness_product, low_mutation_energy_fitness$version ) # J x diff_fitness
low_mutation_energy_fitness_score   <- setNames( low_mutation_energy_fitness$energy_fitness_score, low_mutation_energy_fitness$version )   # log10 of the product

# low_mutation vs microopt: ratio of the products and difference of the scores
low_mutation_energy_fitness_change       <- energy_fitness_change( low_mutation_energy_fitness, "microopt", "low_mutation" )
low_mutation_energy_fitness_ratio        <- low_mutation_energy_fitness_change[["ratio"]]
low_mutation_energy_fitness_score_change <- low_mutation_energy_fitness_change[["score_change"]]

# Referenced as Table \ref{tab:smartgreens.low.mutation.energy.fitness}
energy_fitness_kable( low_mutation_energy_fitness,
  caption = "Median workload energy ($\\Delta$PKG), median distance to the optimum ({\\sf diff\\_fitness}) and energy-fitness score, $\\log_{10}$(energy $\\times$ {\\sf diff\\_fitness}), for {\\sf microopt} and {\\sf low\\_mutation}. Lower is better in all columns." )
@
%

Table \ref{tab:smartgreens.low.mutation.energy.fitness} summarizes the previous figure, showing median workload energy in Joules as well as median fitness difference, together with the logarithmic score. The difference \Sexpr{low_mutation_energy_fitness_score_change} highlights the overall improvement achieved despite having spent more energy in the process (almost 50\% more) in the process.
%
<<smartgreens.low.mutation.operating.conditions, message=FALSE, echo=FALSE>>=
# Same analysis as smartgreens.microopt.operating.conditions, microopt as the
# reference. Savings are positive when low_mutation spends less, negative when
# it spends more.
low_mutation_operating <- operating_conditions( comparison_microopt_low_mutation_w, "microopt", "low_mutation" )

low_mutation_seconds_saved                 <- low_mutation_operating$seconds_saved                 # s per run
low_mutation_energy_saved                  <- low_mutation_operating$energy_saved                  # J per run
low_mutation_energy_saved_same_time        <- low_mutation_operating$energy_saved_same_time        # J, power
low_mutation_energy_saved_through_time     <- low_mutation_operating$energy_saved_through_time     # J, running time
low_mutation_percentage_saved_through_time <- low_mutation_operating$percentage_saved_through_time

# Named microopt, low_mutation, difference_p_value
low_mutation_power                       <- low_mutation_operating$power                       # J per s of running time (W)
low_mutation_seconds_per_degree          <- low_mutation_operating$seconds_per_degree          # s per degree C
low_mutation_energy_per_degree           <- low_mutation_operating$energy_per_degree           # J per degree C, total
low_mutation_energy_per_degree_same_time <- low_mutation_operating$energy_per_degree_same_time # J per degree C, same running time
low_mutation_seconds                     <- low_mutation_operating$seconds                     # median s
low_mutation_initial_temp                <- low_mutation_operating$initial_temp                # median degrees C

# Referenced as Table \ref{tab:smartgreens.low.mutation.operating.conditions}
operating_conditions_kable( low_mutation_operating, "microopt", "low_mutation",
  caption = "Influence of the operating conditions on the energy ($\\Delta$PKG) of a workload run, for {\\sf microopt} and {\\sf low\\_mutation}, with the same algorithm configuration and number of evaluations. Only the values that differ significantly between the two versions ($p < 0.05$) are shown." )
@
%
Table \ref{tab:smartgreens.low.mutation.operating.conditions} explains why more energy is spent in the {\sf low\_mutation} experiment level: workload runs operate at a higher temperature and they take a slightly longer time. Although energy per degree of initial temperature or per degree at the same running time is lower, the fact is that they run longer starting at a higher temperature. $B$ and $W$ runs take longer, thus leave the processor a bit hotter, making the next one spend more energy. What we learn from the optimizations performed so far is that optimizing for performance has a double effect: they take less time, so they do not heat the processor so much. The opposite is also true, which will imply that, in general, there is no free lunch: achieving better fitness will mean obtaining more samples of the space, which will make eventually the algorithm run for longer. At any rate, the balance is positive, as exemplified by the improvement of the energy/fitness score.

<<smartgreens.low.mutation.run.length, message=FALSE, echo=FALSE>>=
# Is the additional energy spent by low_mutation (with respect to microopt)
# spent through longer runs?
#   more generations -> more evaluations -> more running time -> more energy
# This chunk: links of the chain. Next chunk: mediation of the additional
# energy. Code: R/run_length_chain.R.
source("R/run_length_chain.R")
low_mutation_chain <- run_length_chain( comparison_microopt_low_mutation_w, "microopt", "low_mutation" )

# Version effects, low_mutation - microopt, same configuration (estimate, lower, upper, p_value)
low_mutation_additional_generations <- low_mutation_chain$version_effects$generations # per run
low_mutation_additional_evaluations <- low_mutation_chain$version_effects$evaluations # per run
low_mutation_additional_seconds     <- low_mutation_chain$version_effects$seconds     # s per run, same initial temperature
low_mutation_additional_energy      <- low_mutation_chain$version_effects$energy      # J per run, same initial temperature
low_mutation_additional_seconds_overall <- low_mutation_chain$version_effects$seconds_overall # s per run, any initial temperature
low_mutation_additional_energy_overall  <- low_mutation_chain$version_effects$energy_overall  # J per run, any initial temperature (includes carry-over heating)

# Links of the chain (estimate, lower, upper, p_value)
low_mutation_evaluations_per_generation               <- low_mutation_chain$links$evaluations_per_generation
low_mutation_seconds_per_hundred_thousand_evaluations <- low_mutation_chain$links$seconds_per_hundred_thousand_evaluations
low_mutation_energy_per_second                        <- low_mutation_chain$links$energy_per_second

# Referenced as Table \ref{tab:smartgreens.low.mutation.run.length}
run_length_differences_kable( low_mutation_chain,
  caption = "Differences between a {\\sf low\\_mutation} and a {\\sf microopt} workload run with the same configuration, along the chain generations $\\rightarrow$ evaluations $\\rightarrow$ running time ($\\Delta$ seconds) $\\rightarrow$ energy ($\\Delta$PKG). Positive values: {\\sf low\\_mutation} performs or spends more. The last row compares runs that start at the same temperature." )
@

<<smartgreens.low.mutation.run.length.energy, message=FALSE, echo=FALSE>>=
# Additional energy spent by a low_mutation run with respect to microopt,
# starting from the total and adding, in causal order, initial temperature
# (carry-over from previous runs), generations, evaluations, running time and
# final temperature. Percentages: part of the total absorbed by each step on
# its own (negative = the additional energy grows); steps + unexplained = 100.
low_mutation_percentage_through_initial_temperature <- low_mutation_chain$percentage_through_initial_temperature # carry-over
low_mutation_percentage_through_run_length          <- low_mutation_chain$percentage_through_run_length          # generations + evaluations + running time
low_mutation_percentage_through_final_temperature   <- low_mutation_chain$percentage_through_final_temperature   # heating during the run
low_mutation_percentage_unexplained                 <- low_mutation_chain$percentage_unexplained
# TRUE if every link is positive and significant and carry-over + run length are over 50%
low_mutation_chain_supported <- low_mutation_chain$chain_supported
# Additional energy at the same initial temperature, generations, evaluations and running time (estimate, lower, upper, p_value)
low_mutation_additional_energy_same_run_length <- unlist( subset( low_mutation_chain$mediation, controlling_for == "+ running time",
                                                                  c("estimate", "lower", "upper", "p_value") ) )

# Referenced as Table \ref{tab:smartgreens.low.mutation.run.length.energy}
run_length_mediation_kable( low_mutation_chain,
  caption = "Additional energy ($\\Delta$PKG) spent by a {\\sf low\\_mutation} workload run with respect to {\\sf microopt}, with the same configuration. The first row is the total; then the variables are added one at a time, in causal order: initial temperature (left by the previous runs), the run length (generations, evaluations, running time) and the final temperature. Last column: part of the total accounted for by each step on its own (negative: the additional energy grows); together with the part not explained, they add up to 100\\%." )
@

<<ppsn.low.mutation.model, message=FALSE, echo=FALSE, fig.cap="Model for the low mutation change.", fig.height=4, fig.pos="h!tbp">>=
low_mutation_group_temp_model <- glm( initial_temp ~ group + work, data = comparison_microopt_low_mutation )
comparison_microopt_low_mutation$residual_initial_temp <- residuals( low_mutation_group_temp_model )

low_mutation_group_final_temp_model <- glm( delta_temp ~ group + work + initial_temp + seconds, data = comparison_microopt_low_mutation )
comparison_microopt_low_mutation$residual_delta_temp <- residuals( low_mutation_group_final_temp_model )

source("R/process_covariates.R")

comparison_microopt_low_mutation_covariates <- process_covariates( comparison_microopt_low_mutation )

low_mutation_time_model <- glm( seconds ~ seconds_baseline_prev + seconds_baseline_post + group + residual_initial_temp + residual_delta_temp + evaluations + alpha*steps*max_gens*population_size, data = comparison_microopt_low_mutation_covariates )

comparison_microopt_low_mutation_covariates$residual_seconds <- residuals( low_mutation_time_model )

low_mutation_energy_model <- glm( PKG ~ group + PKG_baseline_prev + PKG_baseline_post + residual_initial_temp + residual_delta_temp + residual_seconds + evaluations + alpha*steps*max_gens*population_size, data = comparison_microopt_low_mutation_covariates )

# Type II variance decomposition instead of F/sum(F) on a Type I anova(); see
# R/type_two_variance.R. Note that in the old sequential table PKG_baseline_prev
# came first in the formula after group, so it was credited with all the
# variance it shares with PKG_baseline_post and the operating covariates;
# Type II only credits it with what it explains on its own.
low_mutation_variance <- type_two_variance(low_mutation_energy_model)

# Same as the gamma table: add "other", "shared", "residual" and total rows so
# the percentage column adds up to 100%. Here the listed terms only account for
# ~29% on their own, against an R^2 of ~0.93: most of the explained variance is
# shared among correlated terms (baselines, running time, ...).
low_mutation_variance_top10 <- variance_table_with_totals( low_mutation_variance )

kable(low_mutation_variance_top10, digits = 3, row.names = FALSE,
      caption = paste0("Top 10 significant terms of the energy model for the low mutation change, by Type II sum of squares. Model R-squared = ",
                       round(attr(low_mutation_variance, "r_squared"), 3), "."),
      col.names=c("Model term","Percentage of total variance","Partial eta squared"),
      format = "latex", table.envir = "table*" ) %>%
  kable_styling( full_width = F, position = "center" ) %>%
  row_spec( nrow(low_mutation_variance_top10) - 4, hline_after = TRUE ) %>%
  row_spec( nrow(low_mutation_variance_top10) - 1, hline_after = TRUE )

low_mutation_energy_interaction_model <- glm( PKG ~ PKG_baseline_prev + PKG_baseline_post + residual_initial_temp + residual_delta_temp + residual_seconds + group*evaluations + alpha*steps*max_gens*population_size, data = comparison_microopt_low_mutation_covariates )

# compare these two models using AIC
model_comparison <- AIC( low_mutation_energy_model, low_mutation_energy_interaction_model )

evaluations_model <- glm( evaluations ~ group + alpha*steps*max_gens*population_size, data = comparison_microopt_low_mutation_covariates )
comparison_microopt_low_mutation_covariates$residual_evaluations <- residuals( evaluations_model )

low_mutation_energy_mediated_model <- glm( PKG ~ group + PKG_baseline_prev + PKG_baseline_post + residual_initial_temp + residual_delta_temp + residual_seconds + residual_evaluations + alpha*steps*max_gens*population_size, data = comparison_microopt_low_mutation_covariates )
@

%% "previous B PKG is the top variable" only held under the old sequential
%% ordering, where it was entered first. With Type II, residual_seconds comes
%% first and the two baselines come next. Text rewritten to match; the
%% \Sexpr{} values come from the table rows. -JJ/Claude
Table \ref{tab:ppsn.low.mutation.model} shows the percentage of the total variance (Type II sums of squares) and partial $\eta^2$ of the top 10 significant terms in the model, where we have residualized initial temperature, delta, and seconds. The table also includes the variance that the model explains but that cannot be credited to any single term, because correlated terms explain it jointly, and the unexplained (residual) variance, so the percentages add up to 100\%. Using GLM, we create a model that includes all variables as well as the baseline PKG values, which would account for the framework overhead. The residualized running time is the term that accounts for the most variance on its own, followed by the two $B$ PKG values (posterior and previous), which confirms that the framework overhead must be part of the model. However, most of the explained variance (\Sexpr{round(variance_of_term(low_mutation_variance_top10, "Shared among terms"), 1)}\%) is shared among these correlated terms and cannot be attributed to any one of them. We can already query this model about how much energy we are saving or not with this model; the coefficient is
\Sexpr{round(low_mutation_energy_model$coefficients["groupmicroopt"], 2)}; all things being equal, this is the saving in Joules you will get on average if we do not apply this change. This is a small quantity, however; we must consider that there is a relatively small dependence on the number of evaluations (\Sexpr{signif(low_mutation_energy_model$coefficients["evaluations"], digits=2)}) that, however, is significant only at the \Sexpr{round(summary(low_mutation_energy_model)$coefficients["evaluations","Pr(>|t|)"], 3)} level.

<<ppsn.low.mutation.fitness.table, message=FALSE, echo=FALSE, fig.cap="Fitness vs evaluations, colored by PKG energy spend in Joules.", fig.height=4, fig.pos="h!tbp", fig.env="figure*">>=
comparison_microopt_low_mutation_covariates %>% group_by( population_size, steps, max_gens, alpha, group ) %>%
  summarise( mean_diff_fitness = mean(diff_fitness),
             trim_mean_diff_fitness = mean(diff_fitness, trim=0.2),
             mean_PKG = mean(PKG),
             trim_mean_PKG = mean(PKG, trim=0.2),
             mean_evaluations = mean(evaluations) ) -> summary_comparison_low_mutation
ggplot(comparison_microopt_low_mutation_covariates, aes(x = evaluations, y = diff_fitness, shape=group,color=PKG)) +
  scale_x_log10() +
  scale_y_log10() +
  scale_color_gradient(low = "blue", high = "red") +
  geom_point(alpha=0.5,size=3) +
  labs(y = "Diff Fitness",
    x = "Evaluations"
  ) + theme(legend.position = "bottom") + theme_minimal()
@

So, a price had to be paid for this lunch, although it was not an expensive one. The question turns out to be whether we managed to achieve better fitness in the process, to compensate for this price.
In Figure \ref{fig:ppsn.low.mutation.fitness.table} we plot with different shapes the new baseline, {\sf microopt}, vs. the current {\sf low\_mutation} which uses circles, using log-scaled evaluations as $x$ axis and log-scaled difference to optimum fitness ({\sf diff\_fitness}) as $y$; the color scale stands for PKG energy in joules. There are two distinct {\em clouds} for every shape: the triangle ({\sf microopt}) cloud starts to the left, that is, fewer evaluations, and on top, worse {\sf diff\_fitness}. There is an issue with this block: the slope is very small, that is, more evaluations achieve only a small improvement in fitness. On the other hand, that does not happen with the {\sf low\_mutation} cluster: while
it is slightly redder, more evaluations and more energy expenses, much better fitness is effectively achieved.
% Check the sentence above -Carlos

The model presented above shows that evaluations are not the only factor in energy expenses, and that there are non-linear effects on the operating conditions that cause that, for a similar number of evaluations, {\sf microopt} spends less energy, but at the end of the day what matters is fitness, so we can also look for a certain hue in this graph and see that the fitness obtained for a certain energy expense is always much lower. In general, this graph shows that the {\em low\_mutation} spends more energy because it is running for more evaluations, and this happens because it is able to maintain a high diversity in the population, not getting stuck at a value for {\tt max\_gens} and thus stopping.

<<ppsn.cero.alpha, message=FALSE, echo=FALSE, fig.cap="Comparison of energy consumed vs. final temperature for the microopt and zero alpha set of experiments.", fig.height=4, fig.pos="h!tbp", fig.env="figure*">>=
load("data/ppsn_no_alpha.rds")
ppsn_no_alpha$group <- "no_alpha"
comparison_low_mutation_no_alpha <- rbind(ppsn_no_alpha, ppsn_low_mutation)
comparison_low_mutation_no_alpha[ startsWith( comparison_low_mutation_no_alpha$work, "base-"),]$work <- "Base"
comparison_low_mutation_no_alpha[ !startsWith( comparison_low_mutation_no_alpha$work, "Base"),]$work <- "Work"

comparison_low_mutation_no_alpha$initial_temp <- (comparison_low_mutation_no_alpha$initial_temp_1 + comparison_low_mutation_no_alpha$initial_temp_2 )/ 2

comparison_low_mutation_no_alpha$final_temp <- (comparison_low_mutation_no_alpha$final_temp_1 + comparison_low_mutation_no_alpha$final_temp_2 )/ 2

comparison_low_mutation_no_alpha_covariates <- process_covariates( comparison_low_mutation_no_alpha )

no_alpha_plot <- ggplot(comparison_low_mutation_no_alpha_covariates, aes(x = diff_fitness, y = PKG, color=interaction(group,alpha),shape=factor(alpha) )) +
  geom_point(alpha=0.5) +
  scale_x_log10()+
  labs(x = "Diff Fitness",
    y = "PKG Energy"
  ) + theme(legend.position = "bottom") + theme_minimal()

ggMarginal(no_alpha_plot, type="density", groupFill=T, alpha=0.5)
@

The achieved fitness, however, can probably be improved, so we will try to perform an additional parametric improvement, taking into account that the $\alpha$ percentage has a significant influence on the energy spent, as well as the fitness achieved: we will suppress mutation for this {\em caste}, leaving it to reproduce only via crossover. Additionally, we will differentiate the $\delta$ and $\epsilon$ caste, giving the last one a 20\% probability of changing an element, that is, 2 elements changed for every individual; we will call this configuration {\sf no\_alpha}. What we see in Figure \ref{fig:ppsn.cero.alpha} is a PKG (in J) vs. {\sf diff\_fitness} plot that uses different colors for the two groups interacting with the $\alpha$ percentage.

It is interesting to see that there is not a large difference between the two $\alpha$ values in the {\sf low\_mutation} experiment; however, eliminating mutation makes a difference in fitness, with a higher percentage leading to lower fitness values. But what is more important here is that there are no big differences in energy spent. We will try to dig into the model to effectively check if we have finally achieved a free lunch.

<<ppsn.no.alpha.model, message=FALSE, echo=FALSE, warning=F, fig.cap="Violin boxplot, no-alpha vs. low-mutation.", fig.height=3, fig.pos="h!tbp", fig.env="figure*">>=
comparison_low_mutation_no_alpha_covariates$alpha <- factor(comparison_low_mutation_no_alpha_covariates$alpha)
comparison_low_mutation_no_alpha_covariates$steps <- factor(comparison_low_mutation_no_alpha_covariates$steps)
comparison_low_mutation_no_alpha_covariates$max_gens <- factor(comparison_low_mutation_no_alpha_covariates$max_gens)
comparison_low_mutation_no_alpha_covariates$population_size <- factor(comparison_low_mutation_no_alpha_covariates$population_size)

no_alpha_evaluations_model <- glm( evaluations ~ group*alpha + steps*max_gens*population_size, data = comparison_low_mutation_no_alpha_covariates )
comparison_low_mutation_no_alpha_covariates$residual_evaluations <- residuals( no_alpha_evaluations_model )

no_alpha_initial_temp_model <- glm( initial_temp ~ group + seconds, data = comparison_low_mutation_no_alpha_covariates )
comparison_low_mutation_no_alpha_covariates$residual_initial_temp <- residuals( no_alpha_initial_temp_model )

no_alpha_seconds_model <- glm( seconds ~ group + initial_temp + evaluations + steps*max_gens*population_size, data = comparison_low_mutation_no_alpha_covariates )
comparison_low_mutation_no_alpha_covariates$residual_seconds <- residuals( no_alpha_seconds_model )

no_alpha_energy_model <- glm( PKG ~ alpha*group + PKG_baseline_prev + PKG_baseline_post + residual_initial_temp + residual_seconds + residual_evaluations + steps*max_gens*population_size, data = comparison_low_mutation_no_alpha_covariates )

anova_no_alpha_energy_model <- anova( no_alpha_energy_model )

comparison_low_mutation_no_alpha_covariates %>% group_by( population_size, steps, max_gens, alpha, group ) %>%
  summarise( mean_diff_fitness = mean(diff_fitness),
             trim_mean_diff_fitness = mean(diff_fitness, trim=0.2),
             mean_PKG = mean(PKG),
             trim_mean_PKG = mean(PKG, trim=0.2),
             mean_evaluations = mean(evaluations),
             mean_initial_temp = mean(initial_temp),
             mean_residual_seconds = mean(residual_seconds) ) -> summary_comparison_no_alpha

energy_effort_summary <- comparison_low_mutation_no_alpha_covariates %>%
  # 1. Create a bin for the log10 fitness (e.g., rounds -1.2 to -1)
  # You can use round() or floor() depending on how you want to capture the interval
  mutate(fitness_level = round(log10(diff_fitness))) %>%

  # 2. Filter only for the target fitness levels (-1 through -6)
  filter(fitness_level %in% c(-1, -2, -3, -4, -5, -6)) %>%

  # 3. Group by your experimental group and the new fitness level
  group_by(fitness_level,group) %>%

  # 4. Summarize the PKG (energy) to find the effort required
  summarise(
    median_effort = median(PKG, na.rm = TRUE), # Median is usually safer with scatter outliers
    mean_effort   = mean(PKG, na.rm = TRUE),
    min_effort    = min(PKG, na.rm = TRUE),    # The absolute lowest energy used to hit this level
    n_samples     = n(),                       # Good to know how many data points are in this bin
    .groups       = "drop"
  ) %>%

  # 5. (Optional) Sort it so it reads cleanly
  arrange(-fitness_level, desc(group))

comparison_low_mutation_no_alpha_covariates$fitness_level = round(log10(comparison_low_mutation_no_alpha_covariates$diff_fitness))

ggplot(comparison_low_mutation_no_alpha_covariates, aes(x = factor(-fitness_level), y = PKG, fill = group)) +
  geom_violin() +
  labs(x = "Fitness Level (negative log10 scale)", y = "Median Energy (PKG in Joules)", fill = "Experimental Group") +
  theme_minimal()
@

We have plotted in Figure \ref{fig:ppsn.no.alpha.model} the energy spent per configuration vs. the fitness level reached, where the latter is the integer part of the logarithm of {\sf diff\_fitness}. The chart explains why {\sf low\_mutation} is spending less energy: when it ends with a relatively bad fitness level, the energy is distributed around the 350-400 J level. It does not change when the -1 level is reached; in order to reach that level, which is the minimum for {\sf no\_alpha}, the latter needs to spend a bit more energy. However already at the -2 level we see that the latter needs less energy, and that the energy ranges changes relatively little to reach deeper energy levels; summarizing, {\sf low\_energy} spends less energy just because it is stopping after a relatively low number of evaluations at a shallow fitness level; when comparable levels of fitness are reached, {\sf no\_alpha} is, indeed, a winner.

We have thus found a very economical lunch indeed, which can even be free if we take into account that the energy needed by {\sf no\_alpha} to reach the -3 level is lower than the energy needed by {\sf low\_mutation} to reach the level above, -2.

\section{\uppercase{Discussion and Conclusions}}\label{sec:conclusions}

We all know that there is no free lunch \cite{wolpert2002no}: if you want better fitness, you need to search a bigger part of the search space, or do it more efficiently, but the operators that perform that efficient search come at a cost. However, we can always look for a very economic lunch: try to obtain better fitness levels with a small energetic investment. This is what we have achieved through a sequence of changes in the Brave New Algorithm, a stratified evolutionary algorithm.

%%Simplified the structure. Check. -Carlos
While that has been the final objective, we have had to set up an experimental framework and analysis that effectively proves that is the case. This experimental framework firstly relies on examining operating conditions (mainly temperature), setting up a framework that interleaves {\em baseline} (no workload) and workload experiments, and repeats executions of the same configuration and set of runs under different conditions, using a {\em run-to-idle} protocol in a multi-die CPU (so that new processes are always run in the {\em hotter} die). This way, we can eventually create models not only to find which configuration consumes the most energy, but also which factors among the operating conditions, experimental interventions and algorithm parameters contribute the most to energy consumption and in which direction. This latter goal is accomplished using residualized models that separate the interaction among the different experimental variables.

%%Minor edits here -Carlos
One of the innovations included in this paper is to include the baseline runs in the model, instead of subtracting averages from the workload; the model shows a strong dependence that {\em explains} a good percentage of the variance, but the modelling allows us to estimate precisely by how much, and thus compare the amount of energy spent only by the workload; this avoids unrealistic scenarios where subtracting averages of baselines from the workload lead to very small or even negative energy values \cite{merelo25}.

%%Maybe this paragraph does not need to mention the algorithmic details (describe din detail in the experimentation), and only the results - Carlos
Using this experimental framework and analysis, we have been able to discard a specific change in the algorithm and eventually adopt a parametrization
%leaving the $\alpha$ caste only with crossover, and giving one of the lower castes a high mutation,
that reaches a $10^{-5}$ difference to optimum fitness with a relatively low energy expense or the order of 10J; however, achieving better fitness levels does not depend strongly on the number of evaluations or the population size, so this provides a path for further, although more limited, improvements in fitness.

The interesting thing about this change is that, first, it is within the spirit of the zen of evolutionary algorithms \cite{Zen}: we let Nature, in this case, Literature, be our guide and we stick to the spirit of the $\alpha$ caste by not making any random change in the genetic make-up they leave to their offspring; second, we tip the balance towards exploitation and at the same time we save energy by {\em not} applying one specific operator to a percentage of the population.

Although the main point of the paper has been well proved by using a single BBOB function, it would be interesting to work with other, more energy-demanding functions, as was found in \cite{merelo25}. The energy consumption model will most likely change, giving the number of evaluations a greater influence on the final profile. By how much, however, remains to be seen and measured.
On the other hand, this methodology of researching energy efficiency together with online performance positions the Brave New Algorithm to achieve more competitive results can be achieved, so an investigation into its possibilities in the BBOB suite is already within reach. This, however, is left as future work.


